4. Hash Diagram

The adjacency list can be stored in a hash map. The key is the vertex and the value is the list of its neighbours. The hash function maps each key to a bucket index, which gives average O(1) lookup of a vertex.

    Hash Function: index = hash(vertex) % table_size

    +-------+-----+      +-----+      +-----+      +-----+
    | Index | Key |      |     |      |     |      |     |
    +-------+-----+      +-----+      +-----+      +-----+
    |   0   |  A  | ---> |  B  | ---> |  C  | ---> NULL
    +-------+-----+      +-----+      +-----+
    |   1   |  B  | ---> |  A  | ---> |  D  | ---> NULL
    +-------+-----+      +-----+      +-----+
    |   2   |  C  | ---> |  A  | ---> |  D  | ---> NULL
    +-------+-----+      +-----+      +-----+      +-----+
    |   3   |  D  | ---> |  B  | ---> |  C  | ---> |  E  | ---> NULL
    +-------+-----+      +-----+      +-----+      +-----+
    |   4   |  E  | ---> |  D  | ---> NULL
    +-------+-----+      +-----+

Collision handling: if two vertices hash to the same bucket, they are chained together in that bucket (separate chaining).

========================================================================================================

5. Operation Time Complexity

V = number of vertices, E = number of edges, deg(v) = number of neighbours of vertex v.

    +-------------------------------+------------------+------------------+
    | Operation                     | Adjacency List   | Adjacency Matrix |
    +-------------------------------+------------------+------------------+
    | Add Vertex                    | O(1)             | O(V^2)           |
    +-------------------------------+------------------+------------------+
    | Add Edge                      | O(1)             | O(1)             |
    +-------------------------------+------------------+------------------+
    | Remove Edge                   | O(deg(v))        | O(1)             |
    +-------------------------------+------------------+------------------+
    | Remove Vertex                 | O(V + E)         | O(V^2)           |
    +-------------------------------+------------------+------------------+
    | Check Edge (u, v)             | O(deg(u))        | O(1)             |
    +-------------------------------+------------------+------------------+
    | Get Neighbours of a vertex    | O(deg(v))        | O(V)             |
    +-------------------------------+------------------+------------------+
    | Traversal (BFS / DFS)         | O(V + E)         | O(V^2)           |
    +-------------------------------+------------------+------------------+
    | Space Complexity              | O(V + E)         | O(V^2)           |
    +-------------------------------+------------------+------------------+

========================================================================================================

6. Advantages and Disadvantages

Advantages:
- Memory efficient for sparse graphs, O(V + E).
- Fast iteration over the neighbours of a vertex.
- Adding a vertex or an edge is quick.

Disadvantages:
- Checking whether an edge exists takes O(deg(v)) time.
- Slower than a matrix for dense graphs.

========================================================================================================

7. When to Use

- Use an adjacency list when the graph is sparse (E is much smaller than V^2).
- Use an adjacency matrix when the graph is dense or constant-time edge lookup is needed.
